  h2: The condition
  paragraphs:
    - DMD is an inherited muscle condition that affects about 1 in 3,500 boys. A change in the dystrophin gene, on the X chromosome, leaves muscle fibres without a protein that holds them together, so muscles weaken progressively from early childhood. Most mothers of boys with DMD carry the change without symptoms, and their daughters may too.
  source: "[TBC: Part 8 item 11]"
why:
  h2: Why an earlier diagnosis matters
  paragraphs:
    - The first signs, a late start to walking, frequent falls, difficulty climbing stairs, are easily put down to weakness or poor nutrition. In Indian clinics, boys typically present years after the first symptom. An earlier genetic diagnosis lets care begin in time, and lets the family find out whether the mother and sisters are carriers before the next pregnancy.
